The butterfly circled once, then vanished.

Barnaby approached the pool and looked in.

He expected to see the butterfly.

Instead, he saw himself.

His orange fur was dusted with pollen. His whiskers were damp from the mist. His eyes, usually half-lidded with sleepiness, were wide and bright. He looked different. Not because he had changed, but because he had seen more.

He had crossed the forest.

He had spoken to a fox.

